window.LEARN_CONTENT=window.LEARN_CONTENT||{};window.LEARN_CONTENT["a-3494ec8bcd59bd1e"]={"zh":"<p><a id=\"add-ios-app-intents\"></a></p> <h1 id=\"ios\">添加 iOS 应用操作</h1> <p>使用 Codex 让应用的操作与内容可供“快捷指令”、Siri、Spotlight 及新一代由智能助理驱动的系统体验调用。</p> <p>使用 Codex 和 Build iOS Apps 插件，识别应用应通过 App Intents 暴露的操作和实体，将它们接入“快捷指令”和 Spotlight 等系统界面，并为未来更多由智能助理驱动的工作流做好准备。</p> <p><a id=\"best-for\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_1\">适合哪些情况</h2> <ul> <li>已有实用操作或内容、但仍无法被“快捷指令”、Siri、Spotlight 或更广泛系统发现的 iOS 应用。</li> <li>希望现在先开放少量高价值操作，并逐步支持更多智能助理友好型工作流的团队。</li> <li>含有账户、列表、筛选器、目的地、草稿或媒体等明确对象的应用；这些对象可以成为应用实体，而不是只能留在界面内部。</li> </ul> <p><a id=\"skills-plugins\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_2\">技能与插件</h2> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>技能或插件</th> <th>用途</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td><a href=\"https://github.com/openai/plugins/tree/main/plugins/build-ios-apps\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">build-ios-apps（需联网）</a></td> <td>使用 iOS 构建与 SwiftUI 技能添加 App Intents、应用实体和 App Shortcuts，然后验证应用仍可构建，且能正确路由由意图触发的入口。</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p><a id=\"tech-stack-recommendations\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_3\">技术栈建议</h2> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>需求</th> <th>建议选择</th> <th>原因</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>暴露应用操作</td> <td><a href=\"https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appintents/making-actions-and-content-discoverable-and-widely-available\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">App Intents（需联网）</a></td> <td>App Intents 是系统契约，可让应用操作显示在“快捷指令”、Siri、Spotlight、小组件、控制项及新一代面向智能助理的界面中。</td> </tr> <tr> <td>应用数据接口</td> <td><code>AppEntity</code>、<code>EntityQuery</code> 和显示表示</td> <td>小而设计合理的实体层，可以让系统理解应用对象，而不必暴露整个模型层。</td> </tr> <tr> <td>可发现性层</td> <td><code>AppShortcutsProvider</code>，配合清楚的短语、标题和符号</td> <td>App Shortcuts 让用户更容易找到并运行首批开放的操作，无需从头自行组合所有内容。</td> </tr> <tr> <td>验证循环</td> <td><code>xcodebuild</code>、模拟器检查和针对性运行时路由验证</td> <td>难点不仅是编译意图目标，还要证明系统调用某个意图时，应用会打开或路由到正确位置。</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p><a id=\"starter-prompt\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_4\">起步提示词</h2> <p>为系统和智能助理界面添加 App Intents</p> <pre><code class=\"language-text\">使用 Build iOS Apps 插件审查此 iOS 应用，并添加应该开放给系统调用的 App Intents 操作和实体。\n\n约束：\n- 先找出最有价值的用户操作和核心对象，确定哪些内容应能在应用之外通过“快捷指令”、Siri、Spotlight、小组件、控制项或新一代智能助理系统界面使用。\n- 第一轮范围要小。选出少量无需打开完整应用就确实有用的意图；另外添加需要跳转到特定屏幕或工作流的打开应用意图。\n- 只为系统理解并路由这些操作所必需的数据定义应用实体。如果更小的实体接口已经足够，就不要照搬整个内部模型层。\n- 添加 App Shortcuts，让用户更容易发现这些操作；并为 Siri、Spotlight 和“快捷指令”选择恰当的标题、短语及显示形式。\n- 如果意图需要在主界面中处理，就应将结果清楚地路由回应用，并说明应用场景如何响应这次交接。\n- 完成第一轮后构建并验证应用，然后总结目前支持哪些操作、实体和系统界面。\n\n交付：\n- 首个版本建议开放的意图与实体范围\n- 已实现的意图、实体和 App Shortcuts\n- 应用在运行时如何路由或处理这些意图\n- 目前开放了哪些 Apple 系统体验，以及下一步适合支持哪些体验\n</code></pre> <p><a id=\"related-links\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_5\">关联文档</h2> <ul> <li><a href=\"https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appintents/making-actions-and-content-discoverable-and-widely-available\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">App Intents 概览（需联网）</a></li> <li><a href=\"https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appintents/adopting-app-intents-to-support-system-experiences\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Apple 系统体验示例（需联网）</a></li> </ul> <blockquote> <p>如需完整文档索引，请参阅 <a href=\"https://learn.chatgpt.com/llms.txt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">llms.txt（需联网）</a>。在文档页面 URL 末尾添加 <code>.md</code>，即可获取 Markdown 版本。</p> </blockquote> <p><a id=\"make-the-right-parts-of-your-app-visible-to-the-system\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_6\">让应用中恰当的部分对系统可见</h2> <p>App Intents 是让 iOS 应用脱离自身界面后仍能发挥作用的清晰途径之一。不要把应用当作一个封闭目的地，要求用户先打开它、再一路点击；可以使用 Codex 将应当开放给“快捷指令”、Siri、Spotlight、小组件、控制项及新一代智能助理系统体验的操作和对象提供给系统。</p> <p>这既有助于当下的可发现性和自动化，也能为未来更多由智能助理驱动的体验做好准备。如果你的应用已经能够撰写、打开、筛选、路由或总结有价值的内容，App Intents 就能为系统提供一种结构化方式来调用这些能力。</p> <p><a id=\"start-with-actions-and-entities-not-with-every-screen\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_7\">从操作和实体着手，而不是复制每个屏幕</h2> <p>App Intents 的最佳第一步通常不是“把整个应用都镜像出来”。可以请 Codex 找出：</p> <ul> <li>用户希望无需浏览完整界面就能触发的少数操作；</li> <li>系统为正确路由这些操作而需要理解的应用对象；</li> <li>哪些工作流应以特定状态打开应用，哪些则能直接从系统界面完成。</li> </ul> <p>Apple 的 App Intents 指南为此提供了很好的思路：先定义操作，再定义系统所需的实体接口，随后让这些操作能在不同系统体验中被发现和复用。最有用的参考资料包括<a href=\"https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appintents/making-actions-and-content-discoverable-and-widely-available\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">让操作和内容可被发现并广泛使用（需联网）</a>、<a href=\"https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appintents/creating-your-first-app-intent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">创建你的第一个应用意图（需联网）</a>，以及系统体验示例<a href=\"https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appintents/adopting-app-intents-to-support-system-experiences\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">采用 App Intents 支持系统体验（需联网）</a>。</p> <p><a id=\"think-in-system-surfaces-not-just-in-shortcuts\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_8\">从系统界面出发思考，而不只是增加快捷指令</h2> <p>这项能力不止是“加一个快捷指令”。设计良好的 App Intents 接口能让应用在多个地方发挥作用：</p> <ul> <li>“快捷指令”：用户可以直接运行操作，也能把它们组合进更大的自动化流程。</li> <li>Siri：应用可以提供有意义的动词和深层链接，而不只是泛泛地打开应用。</li> <li>Spotlight：应用实体和 App Shortcuts 可以成为可发现的系统入口。</li> <li>小组件、实时活动、控制项及其他由意图驱动的界面。</li> <li>新一代面向智能助理的体验：相比任意的界面操作流程，系统更容易理解结构化的操作和实体。</li> </ul> <p><a id=\"follow-a-real-app-pattern\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_9\">遵循真实应用的设计模式</h2> <p>应用通常采用以下结构会更合适：</p> <ul> <li>设置专门的 App Intents 目标，而不是把意图类型散落在无关的应用文件中。</li> <li>用 <code>AppShortcutsProvider</code> 为撰写帖子或在特定标签页打开应用等高价值用户操作提供入口。</li> <li>为系统需要理解的对象定义小型 <code>AppEntity</code> 类型，例如账户、列表和时间线筛选器。</li> <li>设计意图处理逻辑，使其能顺利路由回主应用场景，这样被调用的意图就能打开正确的撰写流程，或将应用切换到正确的标签页。</li> </ul> <p>对大多数应用来说，我会让 Codex 遵循这样的模式：先建立小型、面向系统的操作层，保持实体接口精简；当意图需要主界面时，再接入可预测的运行时交接。</p> <p><a id=\"ask-codex-to-design-the-first-intent-surface\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"codex\">请 Codex 设计首批意图接口</h2> <p>最有效的提示词应说明应用的核心对象和最重要的用户操作，然后要求 Codex 选择第一批最小而实用的 App Intents，而不是不加区分地开放所有能力。</p> <p><a id=\"practical-tips\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_10\">实用建议</h2> <p><a id=\"expose-verbs-users-actually-want-outside-the-app\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_11\">暴露用户确实想在应用外使用的动词</h3> <p>适合作为首批意图的操作通常包括撰写、打开、查找、筛选、开始、继续或查看。如果某项操作必须经过漫长的应用内准备流程才有用，它可能不适合纳入第一轮 App Intents。</p> <p><a id=\"keep-entities-smaller-than-your-model-layer\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_12\">实体层要比模型层更精简</h3> <p>系统通常不需要你的完整持久化模型。请 Codex 定义最小的应用实体接口，只要它仍能为 Siri、“快捷指令”和 Spotlight 提供足够上下文，以便正确路由并显示操作即可。</p> <p><a id=\"treat-this-as-assistant-infrastructure-not-only-a-shortcuts-feature\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_13\">把这看作智能助理基础设施，而不只是快捷指令功能</h3> <p>即使首个版本只明显改善“快捷指令”或 Siri 体验，更深层的价值在于：应用开始用结构化操作和实体来描述自身能力。与只能通过点击和视图层级表达能力的应用相比，这更便于未来接入系统与 AI 驱动的入口。</p>","en":"<h1 id=\"add-ios-app-intents\">Add iOS app intents</h1> <p>Use Codex to make your app's actions and content available to Shortcuts, Siri, Spotlight, and newer assistant-driven system experiences.</p> <p>Use Codex and the Build iOS Apps plugin to identify the actions and entities your app should expose through App Intents, wire them into system surfaces like Shortcuts and Spotlight, and prepare your app for more assistant-driven workflows over time.</p> <h2 id=\"best-for\">Best for</h2> <ul> <li>iOS apps that already have useful actions or content but are still invisible to Shortcuts, Siri, Spotlight, or the wider system</li> <li>Teams that want to expose a few high-value actions now and build toward more assistant-friendly workflows over time</li> <li>Apps with clear objects like accounts, lists, filters, destinations, drafts, or media that can become app entities instead of staying locked inside the UI</li> </ul> <h2 id=\"skills-plugins\">Skills &amp; Plugins</h2> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>Skill or plugin</th> <th>Why use it</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td><a href=\"https://github.com/openai/plugins/tree/main/plugins/build-ios-apps\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">build-ios-apps (online)</a></td> <td>Use the iOS build and SwiftUI skills to add App Intents, app entities, and App Shortcuts, then validate that the app still builds and routes intent-driven entry points correctly.</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <h2 id=\"tech-stack-recommendations\">Tech stack recommendations</h2> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>Need</th> <th>Good default</th> <th>Why</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>Action exposure</td> <td><a href=\"https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appintents/making-actions-and-content-discoverable-and-widely-available\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">App Intents (online)</a></td> <td>App Intents are the system contract that lets your app’s actions show up in Shortcuts, Siri, Spotlight, widgets, controls, and newer assistant-facing surfaces.</td> </tr> <tr> <td>App data surface</td> <td><code>AppEntity</code>, <code>EntityQuery</code>, and display representations</td> <td>A small, well-shaped entity layer makes it possible for the system to understand your app’s objects without exposing your entire model layer.</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Discoverability layer</td> <td><code>AppShortcutsProvider</code> with clear phrases, titles, and symbols</td> <td>App Shortcuts make the first set of exposed actions easier to find and run without asking users to build everything from scratch.</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Validation loop</td> <td><code>xcodebuild</code>, simulator checks, and focused runtime routing verification</td> <td>The hard part is not just compiling the intents target, but proving that the app opens or routes to the right place when the system invokes an intent.</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <h2 id=\"starter-prompt\">Starter prompt</h2> <p>Add App Intents for System and Assistant Surfaces</p> <pre><code class=\"language-text\">Use the Build iOS Apps plugin to audit this iOS app and add App Intents for the actions and entities that should be exposed to the system.\n\nConstraints:\n- Start by identifying the app's highest-value user actions and core objects that should be available outside the app in Shortcuts, Siri, Spotlight, widgets, controls, or newer assistant-driven system surfaces.\n- Keep the first pass focused. Pick a small set of intents that are genuinely useful without opening the full app, plus any open-app intents that should deep-link into a specific screen or workflow.\n- Define app entities only for the data the system actually needs to understand and route those actions. Do not mirror the entire internal model layer if a smaller entity surface is enough.\n- Add App Shortcuts where they make the experience more discoverable, and choose titles, phrases, and display representations that would make sense in Siri, Spotlight, and Shortcuts.\n- If the app needs to handle the intent inside the main UI, route the result back into the app cleanly and explain how the app scene reacts to that handoff.\n- Build and validate the app after the first pass, then summarize which actions, entities, and system surfaces are now supported.\n\nDeliver:\n- the recommended intent and entity surface for a first release\n- the implemented intents, entities, and App Shortcuts\n- how the app routes or handles those intents at runtime\n- which Apple system experiences this unlocks now and which ones are logical next steps\n</code></pre> <h2 id=\"related-links\">Related links</h2> <ul> <li><a href=\"https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appintents/making-actions-and-content-discoverable-and-widely-available\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">App Intents overview (online)</a></li> <li><a href=\"https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appintents/adopting-app-intents-to-support-system-experiences\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Apple system experiences sample (online)</a></li> </ul> <blockquote> <p>For the complete documentation index, see <a href=\"https://learn.chatgpt.com/llms.txt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">llms.txt (online)</a>. Markdown versions of documentation pages are available by appending <code>.md</code> to the page URL.</p> </blockquote> <h2 id=\"make-the-right-parts-of-your-app-visible-to-the-system\">Make the right parts of your app visible to the system</h2> <p>App Intents are one of the clearest ways to make an iOS app more useful outside its own UI. Instead of treating your app as a sealed destination that only works after someone launches it and taps around, use Codex to expose the actions and objects that should be available to Shortcuts, Siri, Spotlight, widgets, controls, and newer assistant-driven system experiences.</p> <p>That is useful today for discoverability and automation, and it is a strong preparation step for a more assistant-driven future. If your app already knows how to compose, open, filter, route, or summarize something valuable, App Intents give the system a structured way to ask for that capability.</p> <h2 id=\"start-with-actions-and-entities-not-with-every-screen\">Start with actions and entities, not with every screen</h2> <p>The best first App Intents pass is usually not “mirror the whole app.” Ask Codex to identify:</p> <ul> <li>the few actions a user would want to trigger without navigating the full interface</li> <li>the app objects the system needs to understand to route those actions correctly</li> <li>the workflows that should open the app in a specific state versus the ones that should complete directly from a system surface</li> </ul> <p>Apple’s App Intents guidance is a good frame here: define the action, define the entity surface the system needs, then make those actions discoverable and reusable across system experiences. The most useful references are <a href=\"https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appintents/making-actions-and-content-discoverable-and-widely-available\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Making actions and content discoverable and widely available (online)</a>, <a href=\"https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appintents/creating-your-first-app-intent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Creating your first app intent (online)</a>, and the system-experience sample <a href=\"https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appintents/adopting-app-intents-to-support-system-experiences\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Adopting App Intents to support system experiences (online)</a>.</p> <h2 id=\"think-in-system-surfaces-not-just-in-shortcuts\">Think in system surfaces, not just in shortcuts</h2> <p>The opportunity is broader than “add one shortcut.” A good App Intents surface can make your app useful in several places:</p> <ul> <li>Shortcuts, where users can run actions directly or compose them into larger automations</li> <li>Siri, where the app can expose meaningful verbs and deep links instead of only opening generically</li> <li>Spotlight, where app entities and app shortcuts become discoverable system entry points</li> <li>widgets, Live Activities, controls, and other intent-driven UI surfaces</li> <li>newer assistant-facing experiences, where structured actions and entities are much easier for the system to understand than arbitrary UI flows</li> </ul> <h2 id=\"follow-a-real-app-pattern\">Follow a real app pattern</h2> <p>This usually works best when the app adopts a structure like this:</p> <ul> <li>a dedicated App Intents target instead of scattering intent types across unrelated app files</li> <li><code>AppShortcutsProvider</code> entries for high-value user actions like composing a post or opening the app on a specific tab</li> <li>small <code>AppEntity</code> types for things the system needs to reason about, such as accounts, lists, and timeline filters</li> <li>intent handling that routes back into the main app scene cleanly, so an invoked intent can open the right compose flow or switch the app to the right tab</li> </ul> <p>That is the pattern I would ask Codex to follow for most apps: start with a small system-facing action layer, keep the entity surface narrow, and wire a predictable runtime handoff back into the app when the intent needs the main UI.</p> <h2 id=\"ask-codex-to-design-the-first-intent-surface\">Ask Codex to design the first intent surface</h2> <p>The strongest prompt here is one that gives Codex your app’s core objects and top user actions, then asks it to choose the smallest useful first App Intents surface instead of blindly exposing everything.</p> <h2 id=\"practical-tips\">Practical tips</h2> <h3 id=\"expose-verbs-users-actually-want-outside-the-app\">Expose verbs users actually want outside the app</h3> <p>Good first intents are usually things like compose, open, find, filter, start, continue, or inspect. If an action is only useful after a long in-app setup flow, it may not belong in the first App Intents pass.</p> <h3 id=\"keep-entities-smaller-than-your-model-layer\">Keep entities smaller than your model layer</h3> <p>The system usually does not need your full persistence model. Ask Codex to define the smallest app entity surface that still gives Siri, Shortcuts, and Spotlight enough context to route and display the action correctly.</p> <h3 id=\"treat-this-as-assistant-infrastructure-not-only-a-shortcuts-feature\">Treat this as assistant infrastructure, not only a shortcuts feature</h3> <p>Even if your first release only visibly improves Shortcuts or Siri, the deeper win is that your app starts speaking in structured actions and entities. That makes it easier to participate in future system and AI-driven entry points than an app whose capabilities are only encoded in taps and view hierarchies.</p>"};
